description:
Part of #430. Branch `claude/fuzz-upload-retry-model` exists (empty, at origin/master).

Target: `uploadTransactionsWithFallback` (src/services/powersync.ts, exported as `__uploadTransactionsWithFallbackForTest`). The existing powersync.fuzz.test.ts covers only compactBlockCrudEntries/orderedBlockUpserts — not this orchestrator, which has been broken and re-fixed at least SIX times (2b18b676c missing-row RPC rollback, 58140b90d per-tx rejection tolerance, 71bd72efe HTTP-status threading so permanent 4xx don't jam the queue, 267558e29 atomic+idempotent rejection recording, 9fc1b8729 oversized-batch cap, 05fe86074 succeeded-prefix drain killing re-touch amplification).

Harness: drive with N generated transactions and an injected upload fn whose per-attempt outcomes come from a generated script (success / transient / permanent-4xx / ambiguous, mirroring the REAL error shapes the classifier switches on). Track all observable side effects. Properties:
(a) every tx reaches exactly ONE terminal fate (completed-success / completed-with-recorded-rejection / still-pending) — never double-completed, never silently dropped;
(b) a succeeded prefix is never re-uploaded after a later transient failure;
(c) permanent failures never jam the queue — remaining txs still attempted;
(d) ambiguous outcomes never exceed the retry budget before quarantine;
(e) rejection recording idempotent.
Pin the REAL contract where the sketch and code disagree; this is live sync code — report rather than "fix" anything ambiguous.

Read-mode selection can comment and highlight (read-selection gestures in src/plugins/annotations/actions.ts) but not suggest an edit, because the suggest-edit composer (annotations slice 4c, PR #1511) was not on master when read-mode selection landed (#1517).

Once #1511 is merged:
- add one row to readSelectionGestures that checks onOutline(target), then calls openSuggestionComposer(target.block.id, target.renderScopeId, target.range);
- bind Control+Shift+s on both the read-mode action and the edit-mode suggestEdit action (the binding rationale is in #1517's body: Control+Shift avoids the macOS Cmd+Alt and Cmd+Shift reservations and Windows/Linux AltGr);
- expect a small conflict in readHighlights.ts with 4c's rename of the moved-anchor class.

Done when: right-click Suggest edit and Control+Shift+S on a read-mode selection open the suggestion composer on exactly the selected raw range, with a component test like the comment/highlight cases in readSelection.component.test.tsx.

Run the single-client conversion on the first production workspace.

Runbook: `docs/workspace-e2ee-conversion.md` §8: deploys, no wipe shortcut, fleet closed, key custody, name, disk headroom (about 6× content bytes), `cell` ordering, a rehearsal of the conversion. Key-loss recovery (#1302) is not a prerequisite (the owner's call, 2026-10-20).

**Done when:**
- the flow reports done;
- other devices have unlocked;
- the operator `VACUUM FULL` is scheduled and run;
- ad-hoc tables are dropped;
- #1289's clean-up is applied;
- the "safe from" date is recorded.

**Deploy step:** the server migrations, #1294's and the flow's counts RPC, both applied to hosted by 2026-10-20. The upload limits stay as they are unless preflight lists an oversized object.

Answer the platform-retention questions that decide when a converted workspace is actually "safe if breached":
- Supabase backup, PITR and log retention;
- Storage versioning;
- PowerSync bucket compaction / reprocess, and whether either can ever yield a checkpoint missing a row.

Design: `docs/workspace-e2ee-conversion.md` §9 (platform residue), with the clean-up in §8 (runbook).

**Done when** each answer is recorded with its source in §9, together with the concrete "safe from" rule and the clean-up the runbook must perform.

The in-app conversion flow on one device. It reads the server directly: the `workspaces` row for its phase, and PostgREST pages of `blocks` and `blocks_history` for its data. It never reads this device's PowerSync image, which stops advancing while any write for the workspace is held.

Design: docs/workspace-e2ee-conversion.md §6.3 and §3.

**Phases, read on every run:**

- mode `none` → preflight → key → flip;
- `conversion_phase` = `sealing` → recovery bundle (unless this device holds a verified bundle record) → re-seal → verify → purge, which moves the phase to `cleaning`;
- `cleaning` → pin upgrade → attachments → proof → `finish_conversion`. Idempotent and re-run in full every time. The phase only moves forward, so a resume never falls back, and a crash during cleanup resumes from the server's state.

**Every run** holds the `navigator.locks` lock, re-confirms the contract and, once e2ee, opens the server canary with the stored key before sealing (asking for the saved key if this device has none).

**Steps:**

- **Preflight:**
    - owner and plaintext (an early-out);
    - OVERSIZED objects, a warning with a remedy, never a discard: every media block's verified bytes (soft-deleted included) and every raw Storage object (orphans included) whose ACTUAL sealed size, bytes + `BINARY_ENVELOPE_OVERHEAD_BYTES`, exceeds 50 MiB, the bucket's original limit. `media:size` is cosmetic, so don't trust it. The flow lists them with the remedy (raise the project-wide Storage upload limit to at least the bucket's 50 MiB + 64) and continues once the user confirms it's raised. If that's wrong, nothing is lost: the cleaner deletes a raw object only after a verified keyed upload, so a refused one stays and the proof waits;
    - no row already passes the re-seal's "sealed" test (all three content columns envelope-shaped): after the flip such a plaintext row couldn't be told apart from a sealed one;
    - drained queues, attachment records included;
    - a durable key store;
    - the File System Access API (desktop Chromium);
    - a list of media blocks with no hash-verified source (no local copy, and no raw object whose bytes hash to the block's content hash; remote-only objects are downloaded, checked, and kept in this device's byte store so the bundle takes them from there; Storage is untrusted and could change them after preflight), for the user to confirm, since no step after the flip can bring them back;
    - residue, disk and bundle-size confirmations, where the bundle size includes the server history and blocks.jsonl, both measured by a dry run of step 3's paging.
- **Key:** reuse a key record already on this device from an attempt that didn't flip; otherwise mint with the round-trip and durable-probe checks, reveal once and retype.
- **Flip.** A lock-not-available refusal (the flip's NOWAIT lock on `blocks`) is retried with backoff while the flow shows it's waiting for the database. This device's own gate then closes the workspace and shows the flow.
- **Recovery bundle, right after the flip.** One FOLDER, written through the File System Access directory picker (`showDirectoryPicker`), one file per part, with the database copied under its lock. Not a zip: the repo's only zip writer (`fflate`) writes no ZIP64 and the existing verifier refuses archives past 4 GiB, which a large workspace's database plus attachments can exceed. A folder also rules out duplicate paths. It contains:
    - blocks.jsonl: every block row, soft-deleted ones included, read from the server over PostgREST by id, every column verbatim except that a row the re-seal counts as sealed (its own all-three-columns test) takes its content columns from its re-seal record instead: the `before_diff` of its latest plaintext-to-envelope history row, the same record verify uses (after the flip only the re-seal makes that transition, so the latest one is the re-seal's). A sealed row with no record stops the bundle. So the file is plaintext even when re-taken mid-conversion, and taking it never needs the key: until the purge the server alone can rebuild it, which is what lets a key lost during sealing be recovered even if the saved bundle is lost too. It's consistent only if the workspace's history count is unchanged across the paging; otherwise it's re-read. The workspace's block count, read in that same window, goes in the manifest, and the verify requires exactly that many lines with no id repeated, because key-loss recovery tombstones every row the file lacks. This is key-loss recovery's restore source. It reads the server, so no download gap or reconnect window can make it stale;
    - local.db, for device-local history only;
    - server-history.jsonl up to `through` = `coalesce(max(event_id), 0)`, every column verbatim;
    - attachments/<hex digest>: (the bare lowercase hex; the stored `sha256:<hex>` form has a `:`, which Windows filenames reject) exactly one plaintext entry per media block hash (soft-deleted included), whose sha256 is that hash, from the local byte store or the raw object, whichever verifies. one file per hash, named by it;
    - other-objects/<hex sha256 of the object path>: (a Storage path can hold characters, `/` nesting or a length no filesystem takes as one name; a 64-character digest always fits, and the manifest maps each name back to its original path) every other listed Storage object, byte for byte, which the cleaner may later delete;
    - manifest.json: through, each .jsonl part's line count, the server's block count, the content hashes the user confirmed in preflight as having no verified source, and each part's sha256.

  Re-opened and verified, accepting an empty history file when `through` is 0, then `through`, the line counts, the names of the Storage objects it holds and the sha256 of the verified manifest are kept on the device as the bundle record. Before the purge, the re-opened file must have a manifest with that hash and parts matching it, so a different file with matching counts can't stand in. The cleaner needs that record after the purge, so it's cleared only on a purge refusal or once the conversion finishes.
- **Re-seal and verify (#1292).**
- **Purge.** First re-open the saved bundle folder (stored directory handle, or ask the user to pick it) and re-check every hash against the record. If it can't be read or doesn't match, don't purge: clear the record so the next run re-takes the bundle. Then purge. On `KMC01` (a row isn't sealed) the record stays, and the next run re-seals what's left, verifies and retries. On `KMC02` (the bundle is stale: something outside the re-seal wrote history) the flow tells the user, clears the record, re-takes the bundle and retries. A transport error retries.
- **Pin upgrade, attachments (#1291), done.**

**Done when:**

- an end-to-end test against the local Supabase stack converts a seeded workspace, with UI-state writes made throughout;
- a crash at each phase resumes correctly, including after the purge on a workspace with no attachments;
- a bundle moved or edited during the re-seal blocks the purge, and the re-taken bundle holds plaintext for rows already sealed;
- a purge refusal over an unsealed row resolves by re-sealing and retrying, with the same bundle;
- a write during the blocks.jsonl paging makes it re-read;
- a cancelled or truncated bundle blocks the re-seal;
- a flip that commits after a client timeout is resumed under the same key;
- a second tab can't run the flow concurrently.


**Attachment proof outcome:** "passed, with these blocks unrecoverable" is a terminal result: the flow lists them, and `finish_conversion` follows the user's confirmation. The pre-purge bundle re-check also requires the manifest to name this workspace.

**Bundle read-back checks, beyond hashes and counts:** every `blocks.jsonl` row carries the manifest's workspace id, which is the target; every media block in it has its `attachments/` entry and the entry's bytes hash to its name, unless the manifest lists its hash as having no verified source (and that list holds nothing else); no archive path appears twice. Otherwise a self-consistent manifest could omit an attachment whose raw object the cleaner later deletes.

**Cleanup resumed elsewhere.** A device resuming `cleaning` without the bundle record asks for the saved bundle and verifies it (minus the purged server history) before cleaning. No media counts as unrecoverable until the bundle has been consulted.

**History read-back:** every `server-history.jsonl` row must carry the manifest's workspace id, so a paging or filter bug can't substitute another workspace's history while keeping the count and `through`.

**Oversized objects** (2026-10-19, revised): never archived-and-deleted. An earlier revision did that, and it was reverted because it traded a file's only server copy for 36 bytes. The manifest keeps only the hashes confirmed as having no verified source; the read-back allows exactly those to be missing.

Attachment conversion, with a single cleaner on the converting device.

The same pass is also an owner gesture, **Clean up attachments**, available on any e2ee workspace whether or not a conversion row exists. An older client can still upload a raw object after the conversion finishes, and the ciphertext audit flags it. A standalone run has no bundle record, so it converts raw objects and only reports "neither" objects; it never deletes them.

Design: docs/workspace-e2ee-conversion.md §6.3 (Attachments).

- **One classification rule by content**, next to `deriveContentKey`. For bytes `b` under name `n`:
    - raw iff `n == hex(sha256(b))`;
    - keyed iff `b` opens under the workspace key to `p` and `n == hex(HMAC(K_id, sha256(p)))`;
    - otherwise neither.

  Fast path: a name equal to the keyed name of any media block's hash, soft-deleted ones included, is kept without a download. It only decides "don't delete". Protection never depends on what the listing showed earlier, so a converted orphan's keyed copy is kept on the next pass.
- **One verify helper**, replacing the copies in resolver, uploadDrain and uploadRecovery.
- **A complete listing before acting**, paged by name and recursing into folder entries.
- **Raw:** seal, upload keyed, verify, then delete the raw object. On a 409 whose occupant fails verification (a poisoned or wrong-key copy), delete the occupant and upload again; the Storage policy allows that delete for poison correction. A **refused upload** (such as a 413 for an object whose sealed size exceeds the bucket's effective limit) leaves the raw object in place, reported with the remedy: raise the project-wide upload limit to at least the bucket's 50 MiB + 64. The proof then holds `finish_conversion` until the pass re-runs. A raw object is never deleted without a verified keyed copy. **Keyed:** keep. **Neither:** if the bundle archived it under `other-objects/`, re-read that entry immediately before the delete and delete only if it matches the object's bytes; otherwise, or if the entry can't be read, leave it and report it. It never stops the pass, so every raw object is still reached (after a key-loss recovery, objects kept from the old key are "neither" on every run).
- **The proof:** no raw object remains, no object the bundle archived under `other-objects/` remains (an unreadable bundle holds `finish_conversion` until the folder is picked again; a bundle lost for good leaves them reported as residue, never deleted without a copy), and every media block's keyed object, soft-deleted blocks included, is present and passes the shared verify helper (one download of the attachment set; Storage objects are untrusted). A missing or failing object is rebuilt from the bundle's or local bytes. A media block whose bytes exist nowhere (no raw object, no bundle entry, no local copy) makes the result "passed, with these blocks unrecoverable", a terminal outcome the flow shows the user before finishing.

**Done when tests cover:**

- a raw object with no media block is converted and its keyed copy survives the next pass;
- a soft-deleted media block's object is converted and kept;
- a nested object is deleted by its full path only when the bundle holds it;
- a plaintext file starting with `encb:v1:` is still classified by content;
- an object whose sealed size is exactly 50 MiB seals and uploads under the bucket's original limit;
- the proof's conditions, including a corrupt object at an expected keyed name being rebuilt, and a media block with no bytes anywhere yielding "passed, with these blocks unrecoverable";
- resume after a crash;
- a standalone run after the conversion converts a late raw object and deletes nothing else;
- a "neither" object listed before a raw one doesn't stop the raw one from being converted.
- a corrupt object already at a raw object's keyed path is replaced, and the raw object is then converted and deleted.
- a refused keyed upload leaves the raw object in place, reported, and the proof fails on it; a "neither" object is deleted only after its `other-objects/` entry is re-read and matches, and an unreadable bundle folder leaves it in place.

Re-seal pages read from the server, and verify them against the server's own record of the re-seal. No local state.

Design: docs/workspace-e2ee-conversion.md §6.3 (Re-seal, Verify).

**Re-seal.** Page the workspace's rows over PostgREST by id, soft-deleted ones included, and skip rows that are already sealed. For each page: `sealChunk(rows, wk)`, an open-check of every sealed column (a fast fail on key or AAD bugs), then a direct `apply_block_patches` call with all three columns sealed and `base_updated_at` taken from the row just read. Resuming just runs it again. The source must be a parameter: key-loss recovery (#1302) reuses this with a recovery point as the plaintext source.

**Verify.** A row's record is its LATEST history row whose content columns go from plaintext in `before_diff` to envelopes in `after_diff` that open under the workspace key. (PR #1305 already implements it this way; both conditions exclude an older pre-conversion write that only looked sealed.) It's identified by content, not by position relative to the bundle's `through`, so a re-taken bundle doesn't move it.

- Decrypt each current server row and require every content column to equal the record's `before_diff` value.
- A mismatch is re-sent from that value.
- A row with no record refuses the run.
- The other columns are covered by the purge's `changed_columns` check.

**Done when:**

- a property test shows a sealed page opens to exactly its input;
- a page sealed under another key is refused before sending;
- memory is bounded by the page size;
- resuming re-sends exactly the rows still plaintext on the server;
- a crash between a sealed row's commit and the verify still verifies that row;
- a swapped column, and a stale read that overwrote a write in flight at the flip, are each caught and repaired from `before_diff`;
- a row sealed by anything but the re-seal refuses the run.

Clients: a device whose local pin says plaintext while the server says e2ee.

Design: docs/workspace-e2ee-conversion.md §6.2.

This reads the synced `workspaces` row, which reverses the resolver's "pin and key store only" contract. It does so only in the fail-closed direction: the server's mode can make a device defer, never decrypt less.

**Scope:**

- **Materialize defers.**
- **Block uploads are held.** A held entry stays in `ps_crud`, and the core publishes no checkpoint until `ps_crud` is empty. So a held write also stops that device's downloads for every workspace until it unlocks. Accepted, because the gate keeps such writes rare; #1301 would remove the stall.
- **The attachment drain defers**, with a live-mode check before every upload.
- **The gate (`WorkspaceKeyGate`) reads the workspace's `workspace_conversions` row** over PostgREST (no row means no conversion in progress).
    - While it's `sealing`, no device unlocks or upgrades its pin, and the gate shows that a conversion is in progress. On the converting device, that's where the flow resumes.
    - Otherwise it prompts for the key.
    - While it's `cleaning`, the owner's devices also offer to finish the conversion, so a crash during cleanup resumes from the server's state rather than from a local marker.
- **The one-way pin upgrade:**
    1. validate the canary and store the key;
    2. write a marker;
    3. run `upgradeModePinToE2ee`;
    4. run `repo.drainSyncWorkspace` and `runUploadRecovery`;
    5. clear the marker.

  Startup resumes from the current pin. The upgrade is separate from `unlockWorkspaceWithKey`, which pins first and reports a plaintext pin as a storage failure without storing the key.
- **Every attachment address is derived when used**: drain upload, 409 verify, `recoverFailedUploads`'s probe, and the resolver. Each comes from `contentHash`, the live mode and `K_id`; `rec.contentKey` stays the OPFS key only. The resolver's local lookup also tries the raw name, so pre-conversion bytes keep rendering without a re-download.
- **Detection of a device that copied ciphertext**, which is sent to the gated reset rather than repaired.

**Done when tests cover:**

- defer, hold and drain-defer;
- the gate refusing to unlock while the phase is `sealing`, on the converting device too;
- a crash after the pin upgrade but before cleanup finishes reopens the flow from `cleaning`;
- records of every status uploading to the keyed path after the upgrade;
- a pre-conversion local byte rendering after the upgrade with no download;
- a crash at each upgrade step;
- refusal of a downgrade;
- detection of envelope-shaped local content.

Must reach every device before any conversion.

**Gate, `cleaning` on a device without the bundle record:** the finish offer first asks for the saved bundle and verifies it (#1290 owns the check); it doesn't clean from local state alone.

Server half: one migration with

- **`public.workspace_conversions`** (`workspace_id` text primary key referencing `workspaces` with on delete cascade, `phase` text check in ('sealing','cleaning'), `updated_at`): one row per workspace mid-conversion; no row means none is in progress. RLS is enabled with a SELECT policy for members and no write policy, and insert, update and delete are revoked from anon and authenticated, so only the RPCs below can write it. It isn't synced. The flow reads its phase from it; the client gate (#1293) refuses every unlock while it's `sealing`. A table rather than a `workspaces` column keeps the synced row type and the `@projects` checker untouched, and makes RLS default-deny the write guard.
- three owner-only SECURITY DEFINER RPCs:
    - **`convert_workspace_to_e2ee(ws, canary)`.** It first takes `LOCK TABLE blocks IN SHARE MODE NOWAIT`. If any block write is in flight it refuses with a retryable lock-not-available error, distinguishable from a real refusal. Holding the lock means no write that began before the flip can commit plaintext after it; `blocks_require_ciphertext_for_e2ee` reads the mode without a lock. NOWAIT because `blocks` is shared: a waiting lock request would queue every tenant's writes behind it, and anyone can call the flip on a plaintext workspace of their own. It then sets the mode and the canary, and inserts the `sealing` row.
    - **`purge_workspace_history(ws, through_event_id, archived_count)`**, guarded, with a raised `statement_timeout`. It requires the phase `sealing`. Refusals raise one of two dedicated SQLSTATEs, so clients can tell them from transport errors and from each other: `KMC01` means fix the state and retry (a row isn't sealed), and `KMC02` means the bundle is stale (a count mismatch up to `through`, or a history row after it that isn't a re-seal write). On success it deletes every history row of `ws` and moves the phase to `cleaning`.
    - **`finish_conversion(ws)`.** It requires `cleaning`, then deletes the row. The client calls it only after its attachment proof passes.
- the one-way none → e2ee admit in `workspaces_prevent_e2ee_field_change`, only inside the flip RPC (`current_user = 'postgres'` plus a tx-local `km.conversion`). The key-loss re-key (#1302) adds the only other admit, by the same mechanism.
- the attachments bucket limit raised by the binary envelope's overhead (36 bytes → round up to 64), so a 50 MiB plaintext capture can still be sealed.
- an index on `blocks_history (workspace_id, event_id)` for the export and verify.

Design: docs/workspace-e2ee-conversion.md §5, §6.1.

**Done when pgTAP pins:**

- the flip refuses non-owners and non-none workspaces, and sets `sealing`;
- a block write held open on another connection makes the flip refuse with lock-not-available and change nothing; a later write sees `e2ee` and meets the ciphertext trigger;
- authenticated callers can't insert, update or delete `workspace_conversions` rows, and non-members can't read them;
- nothing outside the three RPCs changes the phase, the mode or the canary, and the phase never moves backward;
- e2ee → none and canary changes stay refused;
- the purge refuses, with KMC01 or KMC02 as specified:
    - a null `through_event_id`, or a phase other than `sealing`;
    - when any block row isn't fully sealed;
    - when the count up to `through` mismatches;
    - when any history row after `through` isn't a re-seal write, including over `through` = 0;
- a successful purge leaves zero history rows and the phase `cleaning`;
- `finish_conversion` refuses unless the phase is `cleaning`.

`docs/cloud-sessions.md` installs bd with upstream's `scripts/install.sh` from `main`, which always installs the LATEST release and has no version flag. Cloud sessions therefore pick up whatever bd upstream last tagged, with no decision on this side — the same unplanned-upgrade shape as an unpinned `brew upgrade`, and one that moves the embedded store's schema on its first command. The workspace is on bd 1.3.0 (#877).

Do, from inside a cloud container:
- replace the install step with a pinned one (`go install github.com/steveyegge/beads/cmd/bd@v1.3.0` with the embedded-Dolt build flags from the release notes, or a checksummed download of the v1.3.0 release asset);
- re-run the runbook under 1.3.0 — `bd bootstrap --yes`, local reads/mutations, and the `bd backup` + `git push` handoff route — and update its "last verified" line;
- note the pinned version is the one to bump when the workspace upgrades.

Merging a type-definition block away is now handled — `core.retargetMergedTypeMembership` moves members onto the survivor. DELETING one is not, and has the same effect: every block tagged with it keeps a token that resolves through nothing, so those blocks lose their chip, their lifted properties, and their place in every by-type query, with no visible signal.

`types` is independent stored state, not a derived mirror of content, so nothing re-derives it later. The delete path has no retarget destination, which is why it is a different question from the merge case rather than the same fix.

Unguarded today: `assertNoSeedDefinitionWrites` covers only code-declared SEED definitions (it refuses to tombstone one), so a user-authored type definition can be deleted freely regardless of how many members it has.

This is a UX decision, not a mechanical one — options, roughly:
- refuse outright while live members exist;
- warn with the member count and require confirmation;
- allow it and offer to untag the members in the same transaction;
- allow it silently and rely on an audit surface to find the orphans later.

Whichever is chosen, the member count is cheap to get: the trigger-maintained `block_types` index answers it directly (note it excludes tombstoned rows, so a full count needs a `properties_json` scan for deleted members too).

Move the "is this workspace's registry loaded" check from the agent bridge into the kernel transaction, where the registry snapshot the write runs against is already captured.

BACKGROUND. The bridge now refuses a mutation aimed at a block outside the active workspace (six typed verbs plus run-action). That closes the reachable hole, but it states the rule at seven call sites and answers it at check time rather than write time. Four review rounds on that PR produced a finding class that kept naming the guard itself: a mid-command workspace switch, an id that arrives from sync between check and write, a fallback field that is validated but never used, N serial reads for a multi-select. All the same shape - a consumer approximating "the registry will be right when the write lands" with "it is right now", where every additional moment of asking is a new window.

THE SEAM. TxEngine.checkWorkspace is already called from all 11 write primitives, inside the transaction, and already enforces one workspace rule (the per-tx pin). Repo.tx already captures the property-definition registry snapshot the write runs against. A check there would be:
  - atomic against a workspace switch mid-command, which no pre-check can be;
  - reachable by run-action handler writes and eval, which no bridge-side check can cover;
  - one statement of the rule instead of seven.
Correcting a claim made while declining this earlier: sync arrival does NOT go through repo.tx (materialize.ts writes via db.writeTransaction), so "a kernel throw would refuse arrival" was false. Arrival is unaffected either way.

MEASURED COST, not estimated. A probe condition added to checkWorkspace - refuse when propertySchemaResolverFor(workspaceId) reports registry-not-workspace-keyed - fails 668 tests across 69 files. Inspection of the shape suggests most are harness setups with NO registry loaded at all rather than genuine cross-workspace writes, so the real work is largely:
  1. distinguish "no registry loaded anywhere" (boot, stage-0, most test harnesses) from "a registry is loaded, for a different workspace" - only the second should refuse;
  2. decide the opt-out for deliberate unopened-workspace seeding (tests do this legitimately, and at least one regression test depends on it);
  3. read the check against the tx's CAPTURED snapshot rather than live registry state, or the atomicity gain is lost;
  4. retire the bridge-side guards once this lands, rather than keeping both.

DO NOT start by widening the registry's scope, and do not add a second recognizer - the point of this issue is to end up with fewer statements of the rule, not more. If step 1 turns out not to be cleanly separable, that is a real finding and the answer may be to keep the bridge-side guard and close this.

Blocked on nothing; the bridge-side guard ships first and is deliberately redundant once this lands.

Structural observation from the PR 507 review (six rounds, 25 findings). The two most serious defects found — a manual Retry editing and truncating the user's previous answer, and a manual channel rerun being dropped by the listener as a duplicate — are the same bug in two places, and both come from one design choice.

The deferral path expresses 'this run never happened' by rolling values BACKWARD: agent:attempts is decremented, the query cursor is restored, and the run therefore recomputes the SAME identity as the run before it. That identity collision is load-bearing on purpose — it is what lets a deferred retry converge its ⏳ note onto the real answer, and lets a re-POSTed channel event be recognised as a duplicate.

But an explicit Retry also resets agent:attempts (that counter is the crash budget, and resetting it is the gesture's point), so a deliberate re-run produced the SAME identity as the run it was meant to supersede. Fixing that meant threading agent:asked-at into the identity as a second discriminator — and the fix had to be applied at every site computing identity independently, which is exactly what was missed the first time.

Alternative shape: make run identity MONOTONIC and never rolled back — a counter that only increases per claim — and express 'this is the same logical work' explicitly rather than by identity collision:
- reply key = the monotonic run id, so a retry can never edit or truncate an earlier answer, by construction;
- a deferred run that wants to replace its ⏳ note reconciles the OLD key away explicitly, instead of colliding with it;
- channel dedup keys off a delivery id that is stable only when the daemon deliberately re-sends;
- agent:attempts stays monotonic as a history, and the retry BUDGET becomes a separate consecutive-crash count that a Retry may reset without touching identity.

